{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"tid-1","run_id":"run-1","tool_use_id":"toolu-1","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-tmp/-tmp-work-r7-budget-during-call-2/sess-1/tasks/tid-1.output","summary":"The Keeper of Gannet Rock\n\nFor thirty-one years, Eamon Strand climbed the spiral stairs of Gannet Rock at dusk, wound the clockwork that turned the great lens, and lit the lamp before the sea could swallow the last of the light. He did it in gales that threw spray against the glass and in fog so thick he could not see the railing. He did it the winter his wife died, and the summer his hands shook so badly he needed two tries to strike a match.\n\nThe island was a mile of black granite, a stone's throw of grass, and a single white tower with a red band painted around its middle. Supply boats came every six weeks when the weather allowed. Between them, Eamon had the gulls, the seals, the tide tables, and the logbook.\n\nHe kept the logbook faithfully. Wind, sea, visibility, vessels sighted. Each evening he wrote the same small facts in the same small hand, and each morning he read back what he had written, as if the pages might tell him something he had forgotten. Sometimes they did. In the margins he had begun to add other things: that the cormorants had nested early, that a child's red mitten had washed up on the north ledge, that the lamp had burned well.\n\nThe relief keeper was supposed to arrive in October. The automated beacon on the mainland had been approved for Gannet Rock, the paperwork said, and the Lights Authority would switch the tower off for good. Eamon read the letter twice, folded it, and put it in his coat. Then he went up the stairs and trimmed the wick as usual.\n\nIn November a young man named Tobias came out on the supply boat to learn the work before the transfer. He was quick with the ropes and clumsy with the lens, and he asked a great many questions. Why did the clockwork need oiling every third day? Why did the old man speak to the foghorn? Eamon did not have good answers to all of them.\n\nOn the last night before the boat was due to take Eamon away, a storm came up from the southwest. They climbed the tower together. The wind rattled the glass. Tobias held the lantern while Eamon checked the mechanism, and for once the old keeper's hands did not shake.\n\n\"You could let me do it tonight,\" Tobias said. \"Show me how it's done, then I'll manage.\"\n\n\"You'll manage,\" Eamon agreed. \"But you'll need to know why it matters first.\"\n\nHe pointed through the glass at the black water. Somewhere out there, a fishing boat was working its way home, its captain counting headlands by memory, trusting that the light would be where it had always been. The beam swept out, pale and steady, and far off a answering pinprick of light flickered on the horizon, then another.\n\n\"They don't need the light to be clever,\" Eamon said. \"They need it to be there, every night, without fail. That's all a keeper is. Someone who doesn't look away.\"\n\nTobias nodded slowly and took the winding key from the old man's hand.\n\nIn the morning the sea had gone flat and grey. Eamon packed his canvas bag, tucked the logbook under his arm, and stood a while at the foot of the tower. He could hear the clockwork still turning overhead, a little unevenly, the way it always had.\n\nThe boat horn sounded. Eamon lifted one hand to the tower, the way a man might wave to an old friend, and then he stepped down into the boat and did not look back, because the light was in good hands now, and he had never needed to look back at it anyway.","usage":{"total_tokens":6263,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":7852},"uuid":"uuid-53","session_id":"sess-1"}
